The mortality reduction from volunteering is partly explained by reduced isolation, sense of purpose, and regular activity — three known protective factors against cognitive decline and cardiovascular disease.
Skills gained in volunteering — leadership, project management, cross-cultural communication, crisis response — are consistently rated highly by employers and are often difficult to develop in early-career employment.
Research on post-retirement volunteering shows a 20% reduction in dementia incidence in active volunteers versus non-volunteers, maintaining the cognitive benefits of purposeful activity.
